
==============================================================================
A1. 8/pi^2 as the coprime density among pairs of ODD integers
==============================================================================
8/pi^2 = 0.81056946913870217155
6/pi^2 = 0.60792710185402662866 (all-integer coprime density, for contrast)
ratio (8/pi^2)/(6/pi^2) = 4/3 = 1/(1-1/4): removing the prime 2 from the Euler product
prod_{3<=p<=2e6 odd prime} (1-1/p^2) = 0.810569495361107
  |prod - 8/pi^2| = 2.6222e-8  (tail of the product over p > 2e6 is O(1/(2e6 log 2e6)) ~ 3e-8)
N = 10000 = 10^4 odd integers 1..19999; ordered pairs = 100000000 = 10^8
CITED: Hardy-Wright, Theorem 332 (coprime density 6/pi^2); odd restriction removes the factor 1-1/4
Moebius exact count of coprime ordered odd pairs = 81058757
brute-force gcd count of coprime ordered odd pairs   = 81058757
density = 81058757 / 100000000 = 0.81058757
8/pi^2  = 0.8105694691387022
difference density - 8/pi^2 = 1.8100861297831727e-05  (Mertens-type error O(log N / N) ~ 0.000921 )
contrast: coprime pairs among ALL 1..N x 1..N = 60794971 density 0.60794971 vs 6/pi^2 = 0.6079271018540267

==============================================================================
A2. Binary entropy H(8/pi^2) exactly; the paste's 0.704 is wrong
==============================================================================
H(8/pi^2)  = 0.700278653411826 bits  (10 digits: 0.7002786534)
H(6/pi^2)  = 0.966124241906 bits (contrast)
the p > 1/2 with H(p) = 0.704 is p = 0.8087879981  (not 8/pi^2 = 0.8105694691 )
H(8/pi^2) - 0.704 = -0.00372135
zero entropy would need p in {0,1}; H(p) > 0 for every 0 < p < 1 (strict concavity).
H(8/pi^2) rounded to 3 decimals = 0.700, to 4 = 0.7003

==============================================================================
A3. Counting refutation of the compression claim
==============================================================================
Pigeonhole: number of binary strings of length < L is 2^L - 1 < 2^L = number of L-bit streams.
 L | 2^L streams | strings of length < L | deficit
  1 |            2 |                    1 | 1
  2 |            4 |                    3 | 1
  3 |            8 |                    7 | 1
  4 |           16 |                   15 | 1
  8 |          256 |                  255 | 1
 16 |        65536 |                65535 | 1
 32 |   4294967296 |           4294967295 | 1
 64 | 18446744073709551616 | 18446744073709551615 | 1
2^64 = 18446744073709551616
=> every injective (lossless) code of all L-bit streams assigns length >= L to at least one stream.
   Prefix-free codes on the uniform source: Kraft + Shannon give mean length >= L (CITED Shannon 1948).
suffix lookahead (last m=4 bits first) on all 2^12 = 4096 streams: 4096 distinct images
=> a bijection composed with any code is still injective; pigeonhole bound unchanged.

Tournament layout: n vertices, C(n,2) arcs (bits); a fixed Hamiltonian spine forces n-1 arcs;
free arcs T(n-2) = C(n,2) - (n-1) = C(n-1,2).  Storage per T(n-2) payload bits = C(n,2) bits.
  n | C(n,2) | T(n-2) | overhead C(n,2)-T(n-2) | payload/storage
  3 |      3 |      1 |                      2 | 0.3333
  4 |      6 |      3 |                      3 | 0.5000
  5 |     10 |      6 |                      4 | 0.6000
  6 |     15 |     10 |                      5 | 0.6667
  7 |     21 |     15 |                      6 | 0.7143
  8 |     28 |     21 |                      7 | 0.7500
  9 |     36 |     28 |                      8 | 0.7778
 10 |     45 |     36 |                      9 | 0.8000
 11 |     55 |     45 |                     10 | 0.8182
 12 |     66 |     55 |                     11 | 0.8333
=> the layout EXPANDS the payload by n-1 bits per tournament; ratio -> 1 but never < 1.
inherited: scaffolding audit Theorem 5.1, sum_T h(T) = n! 2^{T(n-2)}; re-checked below for n <= 6

  n | 2^C(n,2) tournaments | sum_T h(T) | n! 2^{T(n-2)} | equal
  2 |                    2 |          2 |             2 | True
  3 |                    8 |         12 |            12 | True
  4 |                   64 |        192 |           192 | True
  5 |                 1024 |       7680 |          7680 | True
  6 |                32768 |     737280 |        737280 | True

Bernoulli(8/pi^2) model: if the T(n-2) free arcs were i.i.d. defects with P(flip)=8/pi^2,
the entropy would be H(8/pi^2) = 0.70028 bits per ARC; total T(n-2) H bits; per node (n-1)(n-2)/(2n) H:
  n | T(n-2) | total bits | per node
  3 |      1 |      0.700 |    0.233
  4 |      3 |      2.101 |    0.525
  7 |     15 |     10.504 |    1.501
 10 |     36 |     25.210 |    2.521
 20 |    171 |    119.748 |    5.987
100 |   4851 |   3397.052 |   33.971
=> 'per node' is not a constant; the only correct per-symbol figure is per arc, and it is a model
   assumption (i.i.d. Bernoulli defects) with no defined link to Collatz orbits or to coprimality.

==============================================================================
A4. Consecutive odd Syracuse iterates are always coprime (density 1, not 8/pi^2)
==============================================================================
odd n <= 100000: 50000 pairs (n, Syr(n)); gcd != 1 in 0 cases
Proof: gcd(n, 3n+1) = gcd(n, 1) = 1 and Syr(n) | 3n+1, so gcd(n, Syr(n)) = 1. Same for the
unaccelerated map: gcd(n, 3n+1)=1 and gcd(2k, k)=k>1 only on even steps, which are not odd pairs.
=> any 'coprime mask' evaluated on consecutive odd orbit values has density 1, not 8/pi^2 = 0.81057.

